            "text": "Based on the metadata provided for the 80 texts in the Corpus of Lake District Writing (CLDW), here is the assessment of the genre balance. \n\n### Genre Distribution Table\n\n| Genre | Number of Texts | Percentage of Corpus |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\n| Travelogue | 27 | 33.75% |\n| Guide | 18 | 22.50% |\n| Poetry | 8 | 10.00% |\n| Essay | 7 | 8.75% |\n| Journal | 6 | 7.50% |\n| Epistle | 6 | 7.50% |\n| Drama | 1 | 1.25% |\n| Epistle and Journal | 1 | 1.25% |\n| Fiction | 1 | 1.25% |\n| Miscellany | 1 | 1.25% |\n| Novel | 1 | 1.25% |\n| Painting | 1 | 1.25% |\n| Prose Fiction | 1 | 1.25% |\n| Survey | 1 | 1.25% |\n| **Total** | **80** | **100.00%** |\n\n*Note: \"Fiction\", \"Novel\", and \"Prose Fiction\" have been kept distinct to accurately reflect the specific metadata tags provided.*\n\n### Commentary on Genre Balance\n\nThe corpus exhibits a **highly uneven and heavily skewed genre distribution**. \n\nRather than a balanced cross-section of all literary forms, the CLDW is overwhelmingly dominated by non-fiction, travel-oriented, and topographical prose. \n* **Dominance of Touristic Writing:** \"Travelogues\" and \"Guides\" alone account for more than half of the corpus (56.25%). When combined with other life-writing and observational genres like Journals, Epistles, and Essays, topographical and travel literature accounts for approximately 80% of the entire collection.\n* **Underrepresentation of Fictional/Imaginative Genres:** Traditional imaginative genres make up a much smaller footprint. While \"Poetry\" maintains a modest presence (10%), narrative fiction (spread thinly across \"Fiction,\" \"Novel,\" and \"Prose Fiction\") accounts for less than 4% of the corpus collectively, and \"Drama\" represents barely 1%. \n\nThis uneven distribution is entirely logical given the thematic focus of the corpus. Because it is specifically engineered to capture writing *about* a geographical region (the English Lake District) between the 17th and 20th centuries, it naturally reflects the historical reality that this landscape primarily attracted tourists, antiquarians, topographers, and guidebook compilers.",
